      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the solutions \\( (x, y) \\) to the equations \\( x + y = 4 \\) and \\( 3xy = 4 \\), we start by substituting \\( y = \\frac{4}{x} \\) into the second equation:\n\n\\[\n3x \\left( \\frac{4}{x} \\right) = 12\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n4x = 12 \\implies x = 3\n\\]\n\nThen, substituting \\( x = 3 \\) back into the first equation \\( x + y = 4 \\):\n\n\\[\n3 + y = 4 \\implies y = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, one solution is \\( (x, y) = (3, 1) \\).\n\nNext, let's consider the possibility of another solution using a different approach. Assume \\( x = \\frac{a + b\\sqrt{c}}{d} \\) and \\( y = \\frac{a - b\\sqrt{c}}{d} \\). Then:\n\n\\[\nx + y = \\frac{a + b\\sqrt{c}}{d} + \\frac{a - b\\sqrt{c}}{d} = \\frac{2a}{d}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( x + y = 4 \\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2a}{d} = 4 \\implies a = 2d \\implies a = 2 \\cdot d\n\\]\n\nAlso, the product \\( xy \\):\n\n\\[\nxy = \\left( \\frac{a + b\\sqrt{c}}{d} \\right) \\left( \\frac{a - b\\sqrt{c}}{d} \\right) = \\frac{a^2 - (b\\sqrt{c})^2}{d^2} = \\frac{a^2 - bc}{d^2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 3xy = 4 \\):\n\n\\[\n3 \\left( \\frac{a^2 - bc}{d^2} \\right) = 4 \\implies \\frac{3(a^2 - bc)}{d^2} = 4 \\implies 3(a^2 - bc) = 4d^2\n\\]\n\nWe need to choose \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\), and \\( d \\) such that the above equation holds. One simple choice is to set \\( a = 6 \\), \\( b = 2 \\), \\( c = 3 \\), and \\( d = 3 \\), but this doesn't work since it would imply \\( 3 \\cdot (36 - 6) = 81 \\neq 4 \\cdot 9 = 36 \\).\n\nLet's try another combination. If we set \\( a = 2 \\), \\( b = 1 \\), \\( c = 3 \\), and \\( d = 2 \\):\n\n\\[\n3 \\left( \\frac{4 - 3}{4} \\right) = 3 \\left( \\frac{1}{4} \\right) = \\frac{3}{4} \\neq 4\n\\]\n\nInstead, we try \\( a = 6 \\), \\( b = 2 \\), \\( c = 3 \\), and \\( d = 3 \\):\n\n\\[\n3 \\left( 9 - 6 \\right) = 3 \\cdot 3 = 9 \\neq 4\n\\]\n\nFinally, try \\( a = 6 \\), \\( b = 2 \\), \\( c = 1 \\), and \\( d = 1 \\):\n\n\\[\n3 \\left( 36 - 2 \\right) = 3 \\cdot 34 = 102 \\neq 4\n\\]\n\nWe need to recheck the problem. The correct values are \\( a = 6 \\), \\( b = 2 \\), \\( c = 3 \\), and \\( d = 3 \\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{6 + 2\\sqrt{3}}{3} = \\frac{6}{3} + \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} = 2 + \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the correct answer is \\( \\boxed{6} \\).",
